-Part A ΓÇö Short Answers
-
-Multistage graph in DP
-A multistage graph is a directed, weighted graph whose vertices are partitioned into ordered stages,
-and edges go only from one stage to the immediately next stage, modeling sequential decision
-processes over phases. Dynamic programming computes optimal costs or paths by propagating
-solutions across stages (often backward from the last stage), since each stageΓÇÖs optimal choice
-depends only on optimal choices in adjacent stages along allowable edges.[2][9][5][1]
-
-0/1 Knapsack definition
-The 0/1 Knapsack problem gives items with weights and values and a knapsack capacity W, asking to
-select a subset that maximizes total value without exceeding W, where each item can be either taken
-whole or not taken at all (no fractions). The classic DP recurrence considers for item i and capacity w
-the better of excluding the item or including it (if it fits), building a table dp[i][w] for optimal
-substructure and overlapping subproblems.[10][11][12]
-
-General backtracking approach
-Backtracking incrementally builds partial candidates in a depth-first manner and prunes a branch as
-soon as it cannot possibly lead to a valid or optimal solution, then reverses decisions to explore
-alternatives. Conceptually, it traverses a search tree of partial solutions, using constraint checks and
-heuristics to cut off failing branches early to reduce the search space.[3][6][13]
-
-N-Queen problem (brief)
-The N-Queen problem asks to place N queens on an N├ùN chessboard so that no two queens share a
-row, column, or diagonal, typically solved by backtracking with one queen per row and safety checks
-per placement. Constraint propagation eliminates attacked positions after each placement, and
-backtracking moves a queen when a row cannot be completed safely, enabling efficient enumeration
-of solutions.[14][7][8]
-
-Traveling Salesperson Problem (TSP)
-TSP seeks the shortest Hamiltonian tour that visits each city exactly once and returns to the start,
-given intercity distances, and it is NP-hard with reductions from Hamiltonian cycle showing its
-complexity. Exact methods (e.g., DP HeldΓÇôKarp) solve small instances, while
-heuristics/approximations are used for larger scales due to combinatorial explosion.[4]
-
